WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:03.780
Not every Harry Potter star made it through
all eight movies.

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:08.660
A few of them were switched out for a wide
range of reasons, including death, run-ins

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:11.120
with the law and embarrassing scandals.

00:00:11.230 --> 00:00:16.640
Here are some of the major Harry Potter characters
who were ultimately recast between films,

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:19.900
and the stories of how those transfigurations
came to be.

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:24.199
Although there are still plenty of Potter
fans who prefer Richard Harris' depiction

00:00:24.199 --> 00:00:28.480
of Dumbledore, the role was nonetheless recast
ahead of Harry Potter and the Prisoner of

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:33.960
Azkaban, after the two-time Oscar winner passed
away in 2002 from Hodgkin's disease.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:38.600
Harris had expected that he might not be able
to carry on in the role forever, which he

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:40.890
first took to please his granddaughter.

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:42.260
He told the press that:

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:46.829
"I'll keep doing it as long as I enjoy it,
my health holds out and they still want me,

00:00:46.829 --> 00:00:50.560
but the chances of all three of those factors
remaining constant are pretty slim."

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:55.380
The part would eventually go to Michael Gambon,
who starred in all six following films.

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:59.629
But Gambon wasn’t the only British star
invited to don the heaping wig and decadent

00:00:59.629 --> 00:01:00.120
cloaks.

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:04.680
Years after the fact, Sir Ian McKellen revealed
that he, too, had a shot at becoming Albus

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:06.580
Dumbledore, but turned it down.

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:07.540
“I couldn’t.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:13.220
I couldn't take over the part from an actor
who I'd known didn't approve of me.”

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:17.840
Harris had reportedly been unimpressed with
McKellen’s career, implying that he was

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:19.240
a "passionless" actor.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:24.009
Despite all this, McKellen added that he sometimes
saw himself on posters which featured Gambon

00:01:24.009 --> 00:01:25.000
as Dumbledore.

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:29.860
When Lord Voldemort first appeared on-screen
in Harry Potter and the Sorcerer's Stone,

00:01:29.869 --> 00:01:32.130
there wasn't a lot for the actor portraying
him to do.

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:36.310
After all, he was mostly hidden behind Professor
Quirrell's turban throughout the story, and

00:01:36.310 --> 00:01:40.299
even when he did emerge his face was superimposed
on the back of Quirrell’s skull.

00:01:40.299 --> 00:01:44.060
Richard Bremmer did what he could with the
role, but wasn't surprised when he wasn't

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:47.439
invited back to reprise his part in Harry
Potter and the Goblet of Fire.

00:01:47.439 --> 00:01:49.310
Bremmer told This Is Northeast:

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:53.459
"The producers spent a long time fighting
to get me so I'm very proud to have been the

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:53.960
first.

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:57.560
But the role is now being played by Ralph
Fiennes, who is an old mate, in the fourth

00:01:57.560 --> 00:01:58.060
film.

00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:01.280
I've been in the business a long time and
it didn't surprise me they just went for a

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:04.560
bigger name, being Hollywood led, and I'm
happy to pass it on to Ralph."

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:09.160
Fiennes was already an A-lister by the time
Sorcerer’s Stone was released, having won

00:02:09.179 --> 00:02:13.670
Oscar nominations for Schindler's List and
The English Patient, so it's no surprise that

00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:16.780
the filmmakers didn't seek him out for that
brief CGI appearance.

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:23.079
Jamie Waylett did an apt enough job as Draco
Malfoy's pastry-loving henchman Vincent Crabbe.

00:02:23.079 --> 00:02:26.680
Like many of his contemporaries in the series,
the young actor was completely new to the

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:30.819
world of film when he suited up as the Slytherin
sycophant, but was gradually given more and

00:02:30.819 --> 00:02:33.180
more screen time throughout his six-film tenure.

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:37.329
By the time Harry Potter and the Deathly Hallows
arrived, however, Crabbe’s other half Gregory

00:02:37.329 --> 00:02:41.780
Goyle was seen standing next to a new Malfoy
lieutenant: Blaise Zabini.

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:45.770
Crabbe was completely out of the picture,
and while the role itself wasn't recast, the

00:02:45.770 --> 00:02:48.730
character was shuffled out of sight to make
room for Zabini.

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:51.600
This all stemmed from Waylett's off-camera
run-ins with the law.

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:55.900
Waylett was arrested in 2009 after he was
caught growing marijuana plants in his mother's

00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:56.400
home.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:00.499
That incident alone was enough to lose him
his prime position in the Potter world, and

00:03:00.499 --> 00:03:04.999
the troubles would only continue from there,
the actor was later arrested again in 2012

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:08.329
for participating in a riot, and this time
he faced jail.

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:12.780
Waylett’s co-star Jessie Cave would later
publicly rise to his defense, but the damage

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:15.800
was still done, and the actor hasn't worked
on a film since.

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:20.540
When the Gryffindor common room portrait known
as the "Fat Lady" first appeared in Harry

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:25.370
Potter and the Sorcerer's Stone, it was celebrated
actress Elizabeth Spriggs who portrayed her.

00:03:25.370 --> 00:03:30.120
However, Spriggs' portrayal was brief and
rather rigid in blithely asking for the prefect's

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:33.969
password, so before the character would return
in Harry Potter and the Prisoner of Azkaban,

00:03:33.969 --> 00:03:36.140
the actress was replaced with Dawn French.

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:40.310
French brought a more comedic element to the
character as she desperately tries to impress

00:03:40.310 --> 00:03:43.860
the Hogwarts student body with her glass-breaking
operatic skills.

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:53.440
"AAAAAAAA ah amazing, just with my voice!”

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:57.319
French earned the role after her Harry Potter
spoof for charity caught the attention of

00:03:57.319 --> 00:03:59.440
director and producer Christopher Columbus.

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:00.620
Columbus told the BBC:

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:05.260
"I think she's incredibly funny and her Harry
Potter spoof for Comic Relief was pretty amazing."

00:04:05.660 --> 00:04:10.280
The casting of the Harry Potter films was
entirely limited to British thespians, a rule

00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:15.150
so strict it even forced producers to turn
down Robin Williams for the role of Hagrid,

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:20.169
but Verne Troyer became the rare exception
when he was cast as Gringotts banker Griphook

00:04:20.169 --> 00:04:22.000
in Harry Potter and the Sorcerer's Stone.

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:26.280
Troyer was the only American actor with a
major role in the film, but the character

00:04:26.290 --> 00:04:31.270
was voiced by his English co-star Warwick
Davis, who also portrayed Professor Flitwick

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:33.090
and another bank teller in the film.

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:37.280
But by the time Griphook became more significant
to the story in Harry Potter and the Deathly

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:40.289
Hallows, Davis had taken over the role completely.

00:04:40.289 --> 00:04:45.389
“Griphook has so much more depth to him
than I've ever been able to get into my work

00:04:45.389 --> 00:04:48.660
as Professor Flitwick so I went in and read
for Dave Yates."

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:52.819
Though it was never reported as the reason
for Troyer's replacement, the actor had been

00:04:52.819 --> 00:04:56.830
caught up in a number of embarrassing scandals
following the first film's release.

